"""An orthographic approximation, deliberately omitting lexical preferences.

Korean consonant-final syllables produce negative pulses: stops more than
nasals or liquids, and split UTF-8 characters more than whole characters.
There are smaller rules for Japanese small tsu, spaces and parentheses in
recent Korean/Japanese text, percent signs after Cyrillic/accented Latin,
Arabic combining marks, and completed Han byte fragments. A colon after
an opening first-token 'Title' label has its own large negative pulse.
First tokens are left at zero; document boundaries reset all state.
